    "result_text": "The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the disposable-domain blocklist rule for owner email, following the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions.\n\n**Change:** Reject `POST /api/owners` (and `PUT /api/owners/{ownerId}`) with **400** when the email's domain is `mailinator.com`, `tempmail.com`, or `guerrillamail.com`.\n\n**Files:**\n- `function/owner/OwnerEmail.java` \u2014 added a `DISPOSABLE_DOMAINS` set; after the syntax check, `normalize(...)` extracts the domain and throws the new exception when it's blocklisted. Placing it here means both create (`ValidateOwnerFields`) and update (`ApplyOwner`) paths enforce it.\n- `escalation/OwnerEmailDisposableException.java` \u2014 new checked exception (mirrors `OwnerEmailInvalidException`).\n- `escalation/OwnerEmailDisposableExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 responds `400` with `{\"errors\":[\"email\"]}`, matching the existing invalid-email handler.\n- `officefloor/escalation/...OwnerEmailDisposableException.yml` \u2014 wires the handler.\n- `ValidateOwnerFields.java` / `ApplyOwner.java` \u2014 added the new exception to their `throws` clauses (required for OfficeFloor escalation).\n\nThe check compares the domain lower-cased (post-normalization), so `X@Mailinator.com` is also blocked, while syntactically-valid non-blocklisted addresses continue to pass.",
